====================================================================
OBSERVATIONS
====================================================================

1. The known false merge splits cleanly.
   The compound class with 5 members — chain-middle edges plus all
   three cycle edges — produces two subgraphs:
     subgraph #1 (3 edges): R(k1,k2), R(k2,k3), R(k3,k1)  — the cycle
     subgraph #2 (2 edges): R(c2,c3), R(c3,c4)            — a chain fragment
   Size differs (3 vs 2), so these subgraphs cannot be the same pattern
   under any reasonable definition. The 1-hop collision recorded in
   ADR 0006 is no longer misleading at the β layer.

2. "Terminal descent" separates into two isolated instances.
   The compound class that merged R(c4,c5) and R(t2,t4) now produces
   two disjoint single-edge subgraphs. They share a fingerprint but
   no identifiers. If a later pattern-naming layer (ADR 0010) decides
   these are "the same pattern," it will do so by isomorphism of
   singleton subgraphs — not by simple fingerprint matching. This is
   structurally a cleaner framing than the flat compound class
   suggested.

3. The star compound class stays as one subgraph.
   Three edges, all sharing identifier `s`, form a single connected
   component. Star-ness is preserved as a unit — exactly the outcome
   β Option 2 was chosen to enable. A pattern-naming layer can point
   to this one subgraph as the "star instance."

4. Class-vs-subgraph ratio is informative.
   - 14 edges
   - 7 compound classes (from ADR 0007)
   - 9 subgraph instances (this ADR)
   The jump from 7 → 9 is where false merges broke. On a larger or
   more tangled graph, the gap would be bigger. It's a cheap indicator
   of "how much did the connectivity check add to raw compound
   classification."

5. Singletons stay singletons.
   Four compound classes had size 1 (chain-head, both tree branches,
   isolated edge). Each produces a single subgraph of 1 edge. The
   subgraph layer doesn't invent repetition where none existed — it
   just splits false over-merges.

====================================================================
WHAT THIS ENABLES / WHAT IT DOES NOT
====================================================================

Enables:
  - Pattern instances are now subgraphs, not flat classes.
  - The chain+cycle false merge is structurally dissolved: any
    mechanism looking at "what subgraph instances exist under this
    compound fingerprint" sees two shape-distinct groups, not one.
  - The set of candidate pattern instances to reason about has
    moved from "compound classes of edges" to "subgraph instances
    within a compound class." The latter is the right unit.

Does NOT enable (deferred):
  - Decide whether two subgraphs represent the same pattern.
    Subgraph equality at this ADR is set-of-edges equality; two
    subgraphs with different identifiers are always unequal even if
    isomorphic. That is ADR 0009's job.
  - Name a pattern as a meta-R instance (commitment 3). ADR 0010.
  - Decide which candidate patterns become named. ADR 0011 (γ's
    first real task).

====================================================================
DECISION / NEXT
====================================================================

ADR 0008 is validated. The subgraph abstraction is in place; sanity
filter (b) is operational.

Next ADR: 0009 — subgraph canonicalization.

Core question: given two Subgraph values with (possibly) different
identifiers, are they the same pattern?

Minimum-first plan for 0009:
  - Compute a canonical labeling of each subgraph (identifiers
    re-mapped to stable abstract labels based on structural role).
  - Two subgraphs are "the same pattern" iff their canonicalized
    edge sets match.
  - First-pass labeling candidate: Weisfeiler-Lehman-style refinement
    using existing Signatures (ADR 0004), which is a known and
    reasonable heuristic for graph isomorphism.

Key questions to resolve when writing 0009:
  - WL is a heuristic: for the small graphs we're mining now it is
    effectively exact, but for larger cases false positives are
    possible. Is that acceptable for the first β pass? (Likely yes —
    the rare collisions can be filtered later by a more expensive
    check.)
  - Canonical form: returned as what? A sorted vector of edges over
    relabeled identifiers is one option; a Merkle-style hash of
    the WL-refined neighborhoods is another. Only one needs to
    support equality.
  - Cost: WL is linear in edges × iterations; subgraphs at our scale
    are tiny, so this is not a performance concern.

Follow-up probe worth considering before 0010: after 0009 runs, do
distinct compound classes produce isomorphic subgraphs? (e.g., does
the 3-edge star ever reappear somewhere under a different compound
fingerprint that 0007 gave it?) If so, pattern identification at
the subgraph level cuts *across* compound classes, which would be
an important finding.
